er updates an existing one, and opens a PR; a human merges it with a squash or merge commit, so the bot-commit check never sees a bot commit outside `.ard/<issue#>/` on main's first-parent history. Because the bot must bypass branch protection on `main`, a detective check audits it: the workflow `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` walks the first-parent history of `main` (the last 500 commits, or everything after the repository variable `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`) and fails the run, listing the commits and paths in the job summary, when a commit made by the bot changes anything but regular files under a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. Bot commits are identified by **committer** (the App's noreply email or login), not author, because a squash merge of a bot PR has the bot as author. If the 500-commit window is full and its oldest commit is the bot's, the check fails closed until `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE` is set, so a bad commit cannot be flushed out of view. It runs hourly as well as on push, because `[skip ci]` suppresses the push trigger. It fails closed when the `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` repository variable is unset. It reads both variables from repository settings and lives in `.github/workflows/`, which the App cannot write. Alerting is the failed run only; it does not open issues or pause ard.\n224:**The lint scanner** (`scanners/lint`, a `sh` script, one `jq` program and a Dockerfile that builds golangci-lint v2 with the image's Go) is Go only in v1; other ecosystems are follow-ups. It runs golangci-lint once per `go.mod` (pruning `.git`, `vendor`, `node_modules` and `testdata`) with the repo's own config, or a human-owned `golangci.yml` in the role directory when present. These flags are always forced after any config, because findings that vanish are autoclosed: `--max-issues-per-linter=0 --max-same-issues=0` (no truncation), `--new=false --new-from-rev= --new-from-merge-base= --new-from-patch=` (a repo config asking for \"new issues only\" would report nothing on a clean checkout), `--issues-exit-code=0 --fix=false --path-mode=abs`, plus JSON output to a temp file. It fails closed: a golangci-lint exit other than 0, a `typecheck` issue (a compile error), a module needing a newer Go than the image's (`GOTOOLCHAIN=local`; toolchain switching is a follow-up), a path outside the repository or with a control character, a malformed baseline, and a missing `baseline` file all exit non-zero with no output. Because the image builds golangci-lint with `CGO_ENABLED=0`, a repo config's `goplugin` custom linter cannot load and fails the run. The fingerprint is `lint:<rule>:<path>:<msg>`. `<rule>` is the linter plus `/<code>` for a check code (`staticcheck/SA4000`, `gosec/G104`), or for `govet` and `revive` an analyzer name (`govet/printf`). `<msg>` drops the code, control characters and repeated whitespace, and replaces every standalone number with `N`, so line numbers and \"complexity 31\" versus \"32\" never move a fingerprint; a fingerprint over 512 bytes has its message cut by runes. Identical fingerprints in one file collapse into one finding listing every line; the known gap is that a new occurrence of an identical message in the same file is hidden while the old one is open or baselined (a count-aware baseline is a follow-up). The group is `<rule>:<directory>`, so with `group_size: 20` issues are 20 per rule and directory. Severity has two levels: `medium` for `govet`, `staticcheck/SA*`, `gosec*`, `bodyclose`, `nilerr`, `nilnesserr`, `rowserrcheck`, `sqlclosecheck` and `durationcheck`, `low` for everything else; golangci-lint's own severity is ignored. Messages, source lines and paths are untrusted: they are rendered only inside `text` fences with ``` neutralised, and inline code spans have backticks replaced. Every `//nolint` directive in a `.go` file is itself a finding (rule `nolint`, group `nolint:<directory>`, `low`), so a worker cannot close a lint issue by suppressing it; for the same reason the starter protects `**/.golangci.{yml,yaml,toml,json}`. The baseline is the human-owned file `baseline` in the role directory (a protected path, so no agent can baseline a finding): `#` comments and blank lines are ignored, every other line is one fingerprint, and a finding whose fingerprint is listed is dropped. `ard init` does not ship one, so the scanner records a failure every 24 h until a human commits it; an empty file means no baseline. `scan --baseline` prints a baseline of all current findings (ignoring any existing one), and the exact Docker command is in the Dockerfile's header; the run logs how many baseline entries are unused. Baselining a finding that is open closes its issue as \"No longer detected\" (completed); to suppress it instead, close the issue as not planned. A golangci-lint version bump can change message text and so every fingerprint: bump the version and regenerate every repo's `baseline` in the same human PR.\n330:6. **Two commits per round.** The loop commits the code through the committer's code lane (`Branches.CommitCode`) with the title `[<type>: ]<issue title> (#<issue>)`, the redacted summary (secrets and host paths) as the body and an `Ard-Job: <job-id>` trailer, no `[skip ci]`. `<type>` comes from the issue's `type:*` label (`feature` is `feat`, `bug` is `fix`; `docs`, `chore`, `refactor` and `test` keep their names) and with no `type:*` label the `<type>: ` part is dropped and the title is `<issue title> (#<issue>)`. The prefix is never taken from the plan or the lead's routing, so it depends on a maintainer applying `type:*` at intake. At the end close-out commits `worker-<role>.md` and the redacted logs of every agent step to the same branch (or to `main`, as triage's are, when the job stopped before its first push). `.ard/<issue#>/` never goes through the code lane. A push failure (branch exists, conflict, branch gone, guard) fails the job with no automatic retry.\n337:**Review feedback loops back here.** The `reviewfix` loop (#41) starts a round when every requested reviewer has finished and at least one ended `ard:review:<role>:changes`, backed by that role's close-out record (a label alone does not count). It records a `review_round` event in `_controller.db` (counted when requested, ordered by event ID, local to that database) and removes `ard:worker:<role>:done` from the issue, so the worker loop claims it again. The worker fixes on the same branch and reads `review-feedback.md`. When the fix lands with code changes, every requested reviewer runs again at the new head (their `:done` and `:changes` labels come off), because merge-gating approvals must cover the merged head. A round that changes no code, or one that would pass `max_review_rounds` (default 3), posts one comment and adds `ard:needs-human` on the issue; it is not repeated on the same head after a person removes the label. Removing `ard:needs-human` and pushing a new head caps again at once and never reruns reviewers; to continue, raise max_review_rounds, or fix the PR and remove the ard:review:<role>:changes labels so reviewers rerun. Merger conflict and rebase rounds are a follow-up that will use the same round API (`reviewfix.RequestRound`); until then a conflict, like a red required check, goes to `ard:needs-human`.\n383:- GitHub has computed that the branch does not conflict with `main`. Only a computed `mergeable: false` or `mergeable_state: dirty` is a conflict: it gets `ard:needs-human` and one comment, and a follow-up to #41 will turn this into a rebase round (`reviewfix.RequestRound`) that counts toward `max_review_rounds`. A `mergeable` that is still null or unknown, and a 405 \"not mergeable\" from the merge call, mean GitHub is still computing: the merger retries next tick and never labels\n401:A PR can change state behind ard's back, or stop moving, and the merger sees only ready, open PRs. `internal/prwatch` is a controller loop, not an agent, and takes no slot. Every `intervals.prwatch` (default 5m) it reads ard's PRs (App-authored `worker/` branches with base `main`: draft, ready, merged and closed) and **only observes, records and notifies**. It never merges, updates a branch, re-requests a review or starts a round: those belong to the merger, to #252 (stale reviews), #255 (conflicts) and `reviewfix` (#41), and the loop's effects interface has no such call. It is skipped while the controller is paused, like the merger.\n409:| Head moved, conflicts, requested changes, comments, label edits | Logged only |\n15:// but the merger re-checks every review itself and never trusts the flag. A\n32://  3. Conflict: only a computed mergeable=false or mergeable_state dirty is a\n33://     conflict (ard:needs-human, until #41). A null or unknown mergeable, and\n54://     nothing. Rule types the client cannot evaluate (workflows, code\n// Package reviewfix sends a worker PR with blocking review findings back to\n// its worker, with a cap.\n//\n// The loop runs no job and no agent. Labels carry the live state and the\n// append-only review_round event carries the round count, in three phases:\n//\n//   - requested: every requested reviewer has finished, at least one ended\n//     with :changes (proved by its close-out record) and the worker's :done\n//     label was removed so the worker loop claims the issue again.\n//   - applied: the worker's fix landed with code changes; every requested\n//     reviewer's :done and :changes labels were removed so they rerun at the\n//     new head.\n//   - capped: max_review_rounds was reached, or a round changed no code. The\n//     issue got one comment and ard:needs-human.\n//\n// Events are compared by ID, never by time. The merger's conflict rounds will\n// use RequestRound too (a follow-up).\npackage reviewfix","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KcXMUT38YYWe5vNcmjynbE","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChNCCjAkwpK3wc6cpNCQv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01M19FwEJUGHfhB2AmXDzqoL","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\ndef sub(p,a,b):\n    s=open(p).read(); assert a in s,(p,a); open(p,'w').write(s.replace(a,b,1))\nsub('docs/proposal.md','Merger conflict and rebase rounds are a follow-up that will use the same round API (`reviewfix.RequestRound`); until then a conflict, like a red required check, goes to `ard:needs-human`.','The merger can request a conflict round through the same round API (`reviewfix.RequestRound` with reason `conflict`): a computed conflict at a head with no round yet records a `requested` event and the worker merges `main` into the branch. A conflict round lands when the branch head is a merge commit that includes the round\\'s head (a merge-only resolution changes no code against it, so the no-code check does not apply); otherwise it caps. Conflict and review rounds share `max_review_rounds`, and each event records its reason. This request path is off until part B of #255: the merger\\'s `Rounds` dependency stays unset in `servecmd`, so a conflict, like a red required check, still goes to `ard:needs-human`.')\nsub('docs/proposal.md','and a follow-up to #41 will turn this into a rebase round (`reviewfix.RequestRound`) that counts toward `max_review_rounds`.','and, once the merger\\'s `Rounds` dependency is wired (part B of #255; unset today), it becomes a conflict round (`reviewfix.RequestRound`) that counts toward `max_review_rounds`.')\nsub('internal/reviewfix/doc.go','''// Events are compared by ID, never by time. The merger's conflict rounds will\n// use RequestRound too (a follow-up).''','''// Each round has a reason, review or conflict. A conflict round (requested by\n// the merger through RequestRound) lands when the PR head is a merge commit\n// that includes the round's head; a review round lands when code changed.\n// Both count toward max_review_rounds.\n//\n// Events are compared by ID, never by time.''')\nsub('internal/merger/doc.go','''(ard:needs-human, until #41)''','''(ard:needs-human; with Deps.Rounds set, a conflict round is requested instead)''')\nEOF\nsed -n 30,38p internal/merger/doc.go; make test 2>&1 | tail -15; make lint 2>&1 | tail; git -c core.excludesFile=/dev/null status --porcelain --untracked-files=all"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5760,"cache_read_input_tokens":63040,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5760},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4dfccf11-15eb-4385-96da-9b8dce3becc5","uuid":"d87f120b-4701-4df1-a137-33e4654f6957","timestamp":"2026-11-24T18:03:48.822Z","request_id":"req_011ChNCCiyqhx4QFMDfGJ8i9"}
